+# Atomic writes and batches in an MCP server over REST
+
+The tools in `src/tools/atomic/` let an agent do in one call what used to take one call per object:
+build a dashboard with its tiles, tag or delete many insights, create or delete many annotations,
+or change one element of a nested array.
+None of them needed a backend change: they compose the existing REST endpoints.
+That is also their main constraint.
+Separate REST calls are not a transaction, so each tool says exactly which guarantee it gives instead of claiming atomicity it cannot have.
+
+This note describes the five patterns behind them, written so they can be reused in any MCP server that sits in front of a REST API.
+
+| Tool                                                   | Pattern                                                                 | Replaces                                                              |
+| ------------------------------------------------------ | ----------------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------- |
+| `insights-batch-update`                                | validate first, per-item results, dry run; version check for tag merges | one `insight-update` / `insight-delete` per insight                   |
+| `annotations-batch-create`, `annotations-batch-delete` | validate first, per-item results, dry run, retry-safe create            | one `annotation-create` / `annotation-delete` per annotation          |
+| `dashboard-create-with-insights`                       | validate first, compensating rollback, trimmed receipt, dry run         | `dashboard-create` + N × `insight-create` + `dashboard-reorder-tiles` |
+| `insight-update-query-partial`                         | version-checked partial update (JSON Patch or merge patch)              | `insight-update` with the whole query                                 |
+| `feature-flag-update-conditions`                       | version-checked partial update with the API's own precondition          | `update-feature-flag` with the whole `filters`                        |
+| `survey-update-question`                               | version-checked partial update by content fingerprint                   | `survey-update` with every question                                   |
+
+## 1. Validate everything before the first write
+
+**Problem.** A multi-object write that fails at item 7 has already saved items 1 to 6.
+If the failure was a typo the server could have spotted up front, the agent is left with a half-applied change it now has to find and repair.
+
+**Pattern.** Split every multi-object tool into a read-only validation phase and a write phase.
+The validation phase checks each item as far as it can without writing: the input schema, that each referenced object exists, what would change, and for dashboard tiles that each query runs.
+If any item fails, nothing is written and the response lists every item's problem, not just the first.
+
+**Details that matter.**
+
+- Validate nested values with the _same_ schema the single-object tool uses.
+  The tile query is checked against `insight-create`'s query schema, an annotation item is `annotation-create`'s input, and a patched survey question goes through `survey-update`'s question schema.
+  One schema with two entry points cannot drift.
+- Keep the tool's own input schema small and validate the large nested value inside the handler (`validation.ts`).
+  The dashboard tool takes `query` as a plain object, so its schema stays far below the client registry's 16 KB limit while the check is as strict as `insight-create`'s.
+- Where a cheap server-side check exists, use it.
+  `validate: "run"` runs each new tile's query once through the query API, which catches bad HogQL and bad filters that the schema cannot.
+- Report errors in terms the agent can act on: `tile 1 (Broken): query.kind: Invalid input`, not a stack trace.
+
+## 2. Per-item results, and say what kind of batch it is
+
+**Problem.** A batch that returns only "ok" or one error hides which items landed.
+A batch that claims to be atomic over separate REST calls is lying.
+
+**Pattern.** After validation, writes run best-effort with bounded concurrency (`batch.ts`).
+Every item comes back with a status (`ok`, `unchanged`, `error`, `conflict`, `skipped`, `not_attempted`, `exists`, `not_found`), a one-line `changed` receipt (`tags: [] → ["legacy"]`) or the error, and the batch returns a summary with counts.
+The summary and the tool description both say `atomic: false` and spell out the semantics: validated first, then best-effort.
+
+**Details that matter.**
+
+- Cap the batch (50 items) so one call cannot hold a request open for minutes or burst the API's rate limit.
+  The cap is in the schema, so an oversized batch is refused before any read.
+- Fan out with a fixed width (4 requests in flight) and leave rate limiting to the API client, which already retries a 429 after `Retry-After`.
+  A batch should be a polite client, not a second rate limiter.
+- `stop_on_error` stops starting new writes after the first failure; the rest come back `skipped`, so the agent knows exactly which items were never tried.
+- Make creates retry-safe.
+  A best-effort batch will be retried after a timeout, so `annotations-batch-create` matches each item against live annotations (content, date and scope) and reports `exists` with the existing id instead of creating a duplicate.
+- Make deletes idempotent.
+  An id that is already gone is `not_found`, which does not block the rest: the state the caller asked for already holds.
+- Shape receipts for the output format.
+  The exec tool prints results as TOON, which turns a list of flat, uniform objects into one table row each.
+  Keeping `changed` as a short string, rather than a nested object, is what makes 14 delete receipts cost about 470 tokens instead of a nested block per item.
+
+## 3. Compensating rollback for multi-step creates
+
+**Problem.** A dashboard with five tiles is six or more writes.
+If the fourth fails, the user is left with a dashboard missing tiles and orphaned insights.
+
+**Pattern.** Every write step records how to undo itself in a `CompensationLog` (`compensation.ts`).
+When a later step fails, the log runs the undos newest first, attempts every one even if an earlier undo fails, and produces a report.
+The error the agent receives names the failed step, what was rolled back, and anything that could not be (`ROLLBACK INCOMPLETE … must be removed by hand`).
+
+**Details that matter.**
+
+- Validate first (pattern 1), so rollback is for genuine runtime failures, not for input mistakes.
+- The undo is the API's own delete, which for dashboards and insights is a soft delete, the same as the server's delete tools.
+  An existing insight attached as a tile is detached again with a version-checked update (pattern 4), so the rollback cannot overwrite a concurrent change to that insight either.
+- Be honest about what compensation is not: between the first write and the rollback, other readers can see the partial dashboard.
+  The tool description says so.
+- Create tiles sequentially.
+  Parallel creates would be faster, but the order of tiles and the "failed at step X" report both depend on a known sequence.
+- Return a trimmed receipt (dashboard id, name, URL; per tile its position, insight id, short id and tile id) rather than echoing the dashboard.
+  Tile ids come from each insight's own `dashboard_tiles`, so no extra read is needed.
+
+## 4. Version-checked partial updates
+
+**Problem.** The REST endpoints replace nested arrays as a unit: `filters` on a flag, `questions` on a survey, `query` on an insight.
+A partial update in the MCP layer is therefore a read-merge-write, and a naive one silently overwrites any change that lands between its read and its write.
+That is the lost-update bug.
+
+**Pattern** (`versionGuard.ts`):
+
+1. Lock the object inside this server process (`KeyedMutex`), so two MCP updates of the same object through one process run one after the other.
+   The second reads the first one's result, so both land.
+2. Read the object. If the agent sent `base_version` and the object has moved on, refuse.
+3. Apply the patch to what was just read, and validate the result with the single-object tool's schema.
+4. Re-read immediately before the write and compare a fingerprint: the version marker plus the content of the fields being written.
+   Any difference means someone else wrote: write nothing and return a conflict that tells the agent to re-read and retry.
+5. Write, sending the endpoint's own precondition where it has one, and map the API's 409 to the same conflict.
+6. Where the endpoint keeps a write counter, check that the write moved it by exactly one.
+   More than one means another write landed in the window; the receipt says `concurrent_write_detected` with a warning.
+
+Address elements by stable identity, not position, wherever the API offers one: a flag condition by `condition_id`, a survey question by `id`, a variant by `key`.
+JSON Patch `test` operations let the agent assert a value before changing its neighbour.
+The receipt returns the new `version`, so the next call can send it back as `base_version`.
+
+**Version markers per endpoint** (what the API exposes today):
+
+| Object       | Marker                               | Precondition the API accepts                                        | Post-write detection |
+| ------------ | ------------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------- |
+| Feature flag | `version` (counter) and `updated_at` | `version` (refused with 409 for flags whose `filters.version` is 2) | yes, the counter     |
+| Insight      | `updated_at` and `last_modified_at`  | none                                                                | no                   |
+| Survey       | none exposed                         | none                                                                | no                   |
+
+For surveys the fingerprint is the content of the questions themselves, which any concurrent edit of the questions changes; the `version` the tool returns is a hash of them.
+
+### The race window that remains
+
+Steps 1 to 4 close every race except one: a writer **outside this process** (the PostHog UI, another server instance, a script) whose write lands in the single round trip between the final re-read (step 4) and the write (step 5).
+
+- **Flags.** On a flag that checks `version`, the API refuses our write: no loss.
+  On one that does not, our write is applied over theirs, and step 6 reports it, so the agent is told and can re-read: not silent.
+- **Insights and surveys.** The API has no precondition and no write counter, so in that window our write can replace theirs without either side knowing.
+  The unit test `documents the remaining race window` reproduces this deliberately.
+
+The fix for that window belongs in the backend: an `If-Match`/`version` precondition on the insight and survey PATCH, like the one flags have.
+In the MCP layer, a shared lock (for example Redis `SET NX PX` keyed by object) would extend step 1 across server instances, but it still cannot cover the UI.
+
+### What the concurrency tests show
+
+`tests/unit/atomic-version-guard.test.ts` runs two partial updates to _different_ elements of the same object at the same moment, against an in-memory backend with random 0–3 ms latency per request, 40 rounds each:
+
+| Scenario                                          | Both landed | One conflict | Race reported | Silent loss |
+| ------------------------------------------------- | ----------- | ------------ | ------------- | ----------- |
+| Same process, insight series 0 and 2              | 40          | 0            | 0             | 0           |
+| Same process, flag conditions 0 and 1             | 40          | 0            | 0             | 0           |
+| Same process, survey questions 0 and 2            | 40          | 0            | 0             | 0           |
+| Two processes, flag that checks `version`         | 0           | 40           | 0             | 0           |
+| Two processes, flag that does not check `version` | 0           | ~4           | ~36           | 0           |
+
+The test asserts zero silent losses in every scenario (the split between "conflict" and "reported" in the last row varies run to run).
+Two processes racing on an insight or a survey are not in the table, because that is exactly the window above.
+
+## 5. Dry runs
+
+Every write tool takes `dry_run: true`.
+It runs the whole validation phase, including reads and (for dashboards) query runs, computes the receipt the real call would return, and writes nothing.
+Batch items come back as `would_change`, partial updates as `status: dry_run` with the diff.
+An agent can show the user the exact change before making it, which matters most for destructive batches.
+
+## Measured effect on the friction-report jobs
+
+Calls and cl100k tokens (responses plus requests) for the same job, before with the baseline tools (from the 2026-11-10 friction ledger, live self-hosted instance) and after with the new tools (through the real `exec` tool against the in-memory backend; each "after" includes one `info` call for each new tool):
+
+| Job                                             | Calls before → after | Tokens before → after |
+| ----------------------------------------------- | -------------------- | --------------------- |
+| 1 five-tile dashboard and reorder               | 15 → 2               | 28,512 → 1,561        |
+| 2 funnel edit: add a step, -30d, rename         | 2 → 2                | 5,052 → 1,260         |
+| 3 tag 10 insights, archive 5, add 8 annotations | 25 → 4               | 16,355 → 3,102        |
+| 4 add two variants to a flag                    | 2 → 2                | 5,134 → 1,358         |
+| 7 change one survey question                    | 2 → 2                | 11,325 → 750          |
+| 10 delete 8 annotations and 14 insights         | 23 → 3               | 19,174 → 1,333        |
+
+Job 1's "before" counts the dashboard-related `info` calls, the six writes, the three prescribed re-queries and the reorder; the exploratory `query-*` calls are the same on both sides and left out.
+Most of the token saving on jobs 2, 4 and 7 is the `info` call: the partial-update tools document a patch, not the whole object.
